================================================================================
  napp-it CS -- How to read a "status os" snapshot (interpretation guide)
  File: data/howto.ai/status-os-guide.info
================================================================================

PURPOSE
-------
status.pl's "status os" returns a read-only OS status snapshot of a napp-it
node or a Clustergroup member (see status.info for the TOOL reference:
commands, OS detection, particularities). This file is the INTERPRETATION
GUIDE for that output: what each section means, what is normal, what is a
red flag, and how to structure a status analysis answer. Use it whenever
the user asks to analyse/interpret a "status os" snapshot or an OS status
question. The snapshot is live DATA -- interpret it, never invent values;
if a metric is missing or a section did not run (e.g. permission denied),
say so explicitly.

OUTPUT STRUCTURE (per OS family)
--------------------------------
Every "status os" snapshot starts with "OS: <detected>", then the
OS-specific command output:
  Linux/Proxmox: uptime/load, RAM (free), disk (df), failed systemd units,
    boot errors (journalctl -p err -b), kernel errors (dmesg), iostat,
    processes; Proxmox adds pveversion, pvecm status, qm/pct list, pvesm
    status, pve-cluster/corosync/pveproxy errors, ha-manager status.
  FreeBSD: kernel/uptime, CPUs/RAM (top/vmstat/swapinfo), disk, zpool,
    iostat, netstat, kernel errors (dmesg), /var/log/messages, FAILED rc.d
    services, auth failures, CPU temperatures, processes.
  illumos/Solaris: RAM (prtconf/prstat/swap), disk, zpool, iostat -xn,
    netstat, kernel errors, /var/adm/messages, the fmd fault log
    (fmadm faulty, fmdump -v), SMF problems (svcs -xv), auth failures,
    prstat top-processes.
  macOS: version (sw_vers), CPU/RAM (sysctl/vm_stat), top, disk, iostat -d,
    netstat, unified-log errors (log show), dmesg, launchd services,
    logins (last), processes.
  Windows: OS caption/version/build/uptime/RAM, CPU, stopped auto
    services, top-15 processes + perl.exe daemons, System event-log errors
    (1h), Kernel-Power events (reboot/crash), Application event-log errors
    (1h), network adapter errors/discards.

INTERPRETATION BY AREA
----------------------
1. Uptime / load
   - Load average above ~1.0 per core sustained = overloaded; a short load
     spike after boot is normal. Very short uptime (< 10 min) often means
     a recent crash/reboot -- cross-check Kernel-Power (Windows), dmesg/
     journalctl (Linux), fmd (illumos) for the cause.
2. Memory / swap
   - Little free RAM alone is NOT a problem on Linux (page cache); watch
     "available" (free -h) / swap usage / %used in top/prstat instead.
     Persistent swap I/O or high prstat/ps RSS of webserver.pl, server.pl,
     monitor.pl = memory pressure (see status.info: monitor tracks their
     RSS against limits).
3. Disk usage / ZFS (pool data comes from "status pool", but df shows
   overflow risk)
   - Filesystem at/above ~90-95 % = risk; napp-it keeps tmp/ and
     logs in /opt/csweb-gui/tmp. On ZFS members, prefer "status pool"
     (capacity, vdev health, CKSUM) over df.
4. CPU / processes
   - Top-15 by memory: the napp-it daemons (webserver.pl, server.pl,
     monitor.pl, watchdog.pl / perl.exe on Windows) should be present and
     bounded; a missing daemon or one process pinned at ~100 % CPU is a
     red flag.
5. I/O
6. Network
   - netstat -i / Get-NetAdapterStatistics: RX/TX errors, drops, colls
     growing = link/MTU/driver issue. interface DOWN = service outage.
7. Kernel / system logs
   - Linux: journalctl -p err -b + dmesg -- OOM-killer, I/O errors (ext4/
     XFS/nvme), device resets, hung tasks, firmware errors are red flags.
   - Proxmox: corosync/pve-cluster errors = cluster/Quorum problems;
     pvesm inactive = storage missing for guests.
   - illumos/Solaris: fmadm faulty shows ACTIVE hardware faults (highest
     priority), fmdump -v the history; svcs -xv lists maintenance/offline
     SMF services.
   - macOS: log show --level error; look for repeated panic/kernel/crash
     events and disk arbitration errors.
   - Windows: System Level 1/2 (1h), Kernel-Power events 41 (unexpected
     power loss) / 1074 (clean reboot + who/why) / 6008 (dirty shutdown),
     Application Level 1/2 (1h) = app crashes.
8. Services / daemons
   - systemctl --failed (Linux/PVE), FAILED rc.d loop (FreeBSD),
     svcs -xv (illumos), launchctl non-apple (macOS), stopped Automatic
     services (Windows) -- investigate every failing service, not only
     the napp-it ones.
9. napp-it specifics
   - The web-GUI daemons should run: webserver.pl (HTTP), server.pl
     (socket backend :45000), monitor.pl (status checks), watchdog.pl.
     Repeated watchdog "webserver unresponsive ... killing" entries or
     "socket auth wrong" errors (see status cs / error.log) point to
     auth/credential or stale-daemon problems.

RED FLAGS (cross-OS)
--------------------
- Any critical/kernel/fault-log entry: OOM, I/O error, disk reset,
  corosync/pve-cluster error, fmadm fault, Kernel-Power 41, dirty
  shutdown, hardware/firmware warning.
- A stopped/failed napp-it daemon (webserver.pl / server.pl / monitor.pl /
  watchdog.pl or their OS service equivalents).
- Sustained load > cores, sustained %util > 90 %, filesystems > 95 %,
  swap pressure, growing network error counters.
- Very recent reboot without a documented cause.

ANSWER STRUCTURE
----------------
Answer in this order: (1) overall verdict in one sentence, (2) per-area
findings with the concrete numbers from the snapshot, (3) red flags
grouped by severity, (4) recommended next commands (status pool, status
disk, status cs, status member) or checks. Only use values that are in
the snapshot; if the snapshot lacks an area, state that it is not covered
instead of guessing.

   - iostat %util > ~90 % sustained or avgqu-sz growing = storage
     bottleneck; %iowait high while CPU idle = disk-bound. On ZFS, also
     check zpool iostat (ops/bandwidth) via "status pool".
